WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Every UDP datagram should be validated before it changes program state.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.760
The message boundary is available, but the contents are still external input.

3
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:16.380
The receiver should confirm that the datagram is long enough, short enough, well-formed, and meaningful for the current protocol state.

4
00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.140
Minimum and maximum length checks should happen before reading headers or fields.

5
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.160
A short datagram should not be interpreted as a complete header.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.980
An oversized datagram should not drive excessive allocation or processing.

7
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.360
Length decisions should be tied to protocol policy, not only to buffer size.

8
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:45.480
Version fields, message types, flags, counters, embedded lengths, checksums, and payload formats should be checked according to the protocol contract.

9
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.940
Unsupported versions or flags should be rejected or handled through a documented compatibility rule.

10
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.120
A checksum or integrity field can support validation, but it does not replace bounds and format checks.

11
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:04.360
Unsupported, malformed, truncated, oversized, or out-of-sequence messages should fail safely.

12
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:12.860
That may mean ignoring the datagram, returning a small error response when appropriate, updating a diagnostic counter, or closing related state.

13
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.840
It should not mean guessing missing fields or applying a state change from partial data.

14
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:25.800
Validation should happen before allocation, copying, deep parsing, command dispatch, or generating large responses.

15
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:36.220
Parser code should make protocol assumptions visible: which fields are required, which sizes are allowed, which message types are supported, and when the receiver is allowed to act.
